  "text_md": "  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td> </td>\n∆NH O. .\n−328\n  <tr>\n  <tr>\n</table>\n0.0 0.2 0.4 0.6\n<table>\n  </tr>\n    <td> </td>\n    <td> </td>\n2\n  </tr>\n0\n    <td> </td>\n  <tr>\n    <td> </td>\n  <tr>\n−332\nFIG. 1: (upper) Energies projected to positive parity states, Jπ = 0+ and 2+ states, (middle) the triaxiality γ for protons and neutrons, and (lower) the H.O. values relative to lowest allowed states are plotted as functions of the quadrupole deformation parameter β of the total system.\n</table>\nenergy [MeV]\n  </tr>\n![](<7ef73ffc5cb2ce93f420d68e4a013835298e6d952c7f91c126f198ee7d0a8198_images/imageFile5.png>)\n    <td> </td>\ntriaxiality [deg]γ\n  <tr>\n    <td> </td>\n</table>\nproton neutron\n<table>\n    <td> </td>\nThe values of triaxiality γ for protons and those for neutrons are similar in the entire β region (the middle panel of Fig. 1). In the small β region, the system forms an oblate shape with γ ∼ 60◦, while in the large β region around the SD minimum, it forms a triaxial deformation with γ ∼ 10◦. Density distributions of the obtained wave function at the SD minimum [(β, γ) = (0.478, 11.2◦)] are shown in Fig. 2. Because of triaxial deformations around the SD minimum, each wave function contains diﬀerent K components, and two Jπ = 2+ states are obtained after diagonalizing those diﬀerent K components, i.e., K-mixing\n    <td> </td>\n    <td> </td>\n</table>\n    <td> </td>\n    <td> </td>\n  </tr>\n  </tr>\n![](<7ef73ffc5cb2ce93f420d68e4a013835298e6d952c7f91c126f198ee7d0a8198_images/imageFile6.png>)\n  <tr>\n    <td> </td>",
